strengths_contributions.value:
Traditional MLIP evaluation relies heavily on error metrics (such as MAE and RMSE) against DFT reference values, which do not effectively capture performance under extreme conditions or in real-world scientific tasks. This paper proposes a more physically consistent and task-oriented approach to assess the generalization and practical utility of MLIPs, focusing on the following four categories of metrics:
- Asymptotic behavior far from equilibrium, such as the smoothness of the potential energy surface (PES), short-range repulsion, and the conservative nature of the force field; 
- Stability and reactivity under extreme conditions, using molecular dynamics (MD) simulations to evaluate behavior under high temperature and pressure (e.g., hydrogen combustion);
- Generalization under out-of-distribution (OOD) conditions, assessing energy conservation and rotational symmetry on “unseen” or anomalous structures;
- Prediction of thermodynamic properties and phenomena, such as vacancy migration, phase transitions, and gas adsorption.

